     "note": "Axes, ticks and legends — the furniture. It should be the quietest thing on",
     "body": "# Axes, ticks and legends — the furniture. It should be the quietest thing on\n# the chart and still answer \"compared to what?\".\n\n## tick marks — pick by how much room the axis has\n ├─────┼─────┼─────┤     full: ends and interior ticks\n ╶─────┬─────┬─────╴     open: the data does not stop at the ends\n └─────┴─────┴─────┘     a floor only, when the y-axis is implied\n 0     250   500   750   labels under the ticks, never between them\n\n## how many ticks\n 4 or 5. Three is a hint, seven is a grid. If you need seven, the reader\n needs a table.\n\n## the y-axis — right-aligned, so the digits line up with each other\n  500┤\n  250┤\n  100┤\n    0└──────────────\n\n## a baseline that is not zero — say so, loudly\n   80┤                        ⢀⡠⠔⠊\n   60┤            ⢀⣀⡠⠤⠒⠉\n   40┤⣀⡠⠤⠒⠉\n     └────────────────────────────\n     ⚠ y starts at 40, not 0\n ↑ a truncated axis doubles every slope. Either start at zero or print this\n   line; there is no third option.\n\n## legends — inline beats a key, always\n this year  ▰▰▰▰▰▰▰▰   1.2M\n last year  ▱▱▱▱▱▱     0.9M\n ↑ the label sits on the thing it names. A separate key makes the eye carry\n   a colour across the page and back.\n\n when a key is unavoidable, keep it one line and always in series order:\n  ▰ served   ▱ cached   ◆ failed\n\n## units — in the header, once\n p99 (ms)    486      not:   486 ms, 211 ms, 198 ms down the column\n\n## no data — the axis still renders\n  100┤\n     │            no data for this window\n    0└──────────────────────────────────\n ↑ an empty chart that collapses to nothing reads as a bug\n\n# paint: axes, ticks, rules -> line-0 · labels -> text.muted, mono, small\n# the ⚠ note about a truncated axis -> kaki-1 — it is a warning, not a caption"
