     "name": "quotes",
     "note": "Quotes — a line that is meant to be dwelt on. It earns the space only if the",
     "body": "# Quotes — a line that is meant to be dwelt on. It earns the space only if the\n# page around it is calm; one per page, and never two in sight of each other.\n\n## the pull quote — the serif, the measure, and nothing else\n     Colour earns its power by being rare.\n\n## with an attribution\n     Colour earns its power by being rare.\n                                — the one law\n\n## marked, when the page needs the boundary stated\n ───────────── ◆ ─────────────\n     Gold is the seam, never the slab.\n ─────────────────────────────\n\n## the block quote — someone else's words, kept at arm's length\n │ Nobody would wear a solid-gold watch, but a gilded edge is precious.\n │ So gold lives only as a line, an edge, a frame or a glyph.\n ↑ a left rule, not quotation marks: marks fight the mono grid and wrap badly\n\n## an epigraph — above the thing it frames, smaller than it\n     a design language\n     ────────────────\n     Warm monochrome, struck rarely by colour.\n\n## a terminal quote — a line of output worth stopping on\n ❯ build.py --check\n ✓ clean — output matches canon, every role passes on every surface.\n\n## what a quote must not do\n ✗  a quote in a coloured box            the calm IS the emphasis\n ✗  italic AND larger AND centred AND gold — pick one\n ✗  a quote that repeats the paragraph above it\n ✗  “smart quotes” around a mono line    they break the column\n\n# paint: the quote itself -> text.heading in the serif, 1.4–1.8× body\n#        the attribution and the rule -> text.muted · the ◆ -> text.gold\n#        never a fill behind it, and never a trigger colour on it"
