     "note": "Commits, changelogs and releases — the parts of a project that are read long",
     "body": "# Commits, changelogs and releases — the parts of a project that are read long\n# after they are written. Plain text, no colour available, every time.\n\n## a commit subject — the change, not the activity\n ✓  fix(palette): raise kin-2 so dim gold clears 4.5:1 as text\n ✓  feat(kit): a settings row, and the canon entry behind it\n ✗  updates                        what updated, and why\n ✗  fix bug                        which bug\n ✗  WIP                            then do not commit it\n\n## the body — why, not what. The diff already says what.\n fix(palette): raise kin-2 so dim gold clears 4.5:1 as text\n\n kin-2 was measured only against bg.surface, where it passed at 4.6:1.\n Against bg.app it was 4.33:1 — and bg.app is where labels actually land.\n\n The proof now measures every text role against every surface, which is\n why this was caught at all.\n\n## a changelog entry — one line, one fact, the impact first\n ## 1.2.0 — 2026-06-24\n\n ### Breaking\n · `--y-radius` is now `--y-r-card`. One rename, no shim.\n\n ### Added\n · `.y-row` — a settings row, with the canon entry behind it.\n · Arrow-key navigation inside every tablist.\n\n ### Fixed\n · Nested `[data-theme]` scopes now re-declare the whole role contract; the\n   side-by-side theme demo was rendering half of it in the parent's values.\n\n## a release note — what a person gets, in their words not yours\n yoshiki 1.2.0\n\n The front page is now an instrument rather than a brochure: one live\n specimen you switch, restage and operate in the first screen.\n\n Upgrading is one rename — `--y-radius` → `--y-r-card`.\n\n## a tag and its badge row\n v1.2.0   ◆ stable   MIT   42 tokens · 184 contrast checks\n\n## conventions worth holding\n · imperative mood: \"raise\", not \"raised\" or \"raises\"\n · one change per commit; a commit that needs \"and\" is two commits\n · the subject fits in 72 columns, because something will truncate it\n · a breaking change says so in the subject, not only in the body\n\n# paint: nothing. This is the text that has to survive with no colour at all —\n# which is exactly why the language puts so much weight on structure."
